    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0cb063f4_551ab7e0",
        "filename": "arch/x86/coco/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 138,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-14T14:21:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The INIT/SIPI method is not secure. pKVM hypervisor will not allow it.\n\nThis code is only running if we are running in a protected VM. In all other cases kernel will still use the INIT/SIPI method instead.\n\nBesides, if the hypercall is not implemented, the pVM still successfully boots, although with just one CPU (since it fails to start any other CPUs). Even better, it immediately detects that a CPU is not possible to start (since the hypercall returns an error, and thus pkvm_wakeup_secondary_cpu() also returns an error), so it doesn\u0027t delay the boot by hopeless waiting for the CPU to come online, it just immediately prints an error and proceeds booting.",
      "parentUuid": "07f3d222_4d8deebc",
      "revId": "c53c773a82e5e9bf2253e385d634004ab258f3ae",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bd5eb1c2_b6c328e9",
        "filename": "arch/x86/coco/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 138,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-14T14:38:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah I understand that this is relevant only with protected VMs, was just curious about change in behavior if we have a version of pkvm without this hypercall implemented in upstream scenarios. Usually kvm does capability check for catering to guest and host running different versions. But in our case I understand that all these changes will go as a single feature upstream. So I guess there is no worry of a version of pkvm without this hypercall implemented :-). Thanks for clarification!",
      "parentUuid": "0cb063f4_551ab7e0",
